 ### [Score-based Densification](4705-score-based-densification.md)
-Score-based Densification is a constructive training strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that controls model size by adding new primitives only to those Gaussians that maximize a specific quality score, ensuring the final model adheres to a user-defined budget without requiring pruning [4705-score-based-densification]. Unlike standard 3D Gaussian Splatting which relies on heuristic thresholds and subsequent culling, this method uses a purely constructive schedule that steers densification toward Gaussians contributing most to reconstruction quality, avoiding optimization instability caused by aggressive removal [4705-score-based-densification].
+A constructive training strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that controls model size by adding new primitives only to those Gaussians that maximize a specific quality score, ensuring the final model adheres to a user-defined budget without requiring pruning. Unlike standard 3D Gaussian Splatting which relies on heuristic thresholds and subsequent culling, this method uses a purely constructive schedule that steers densification toward Gaussians contributing most to reconstruction quality, avoiding optimization instability caused by aggressive removal. See [Score-based Densification](4705-score-based-densification.md).
 
 ### [Gaussian Insertion and Pruning](10074-gaussian-insertion-and-pruning.md)
-Gaussian Insertion and Pruning is a dynamic management strategy for 3D Gaussian primitives used primarily in monocular and RGB-D SLAM systems [10074-gaussian-insertion-and-pruning]. It solves the problem of maintaining a coherent 3D structure in real-time by inserting new Gaussians to capture newly visible scene elements and pruning those that are geometrically unstable or contribute negligibly to the render [10074-gaussian-insertion-and-pruning]. Unlike static densification strategies that rely on gradient magnitude thresholds, this method relies on visibility consistency across a sliding window of keyframes and opacity thresholds to manage the primitive set [10074-gaussian-insertion-and-pruning].
+A dynamic management strategy for 3D Gaussian primitives used primarily in monocular and RGB-D SLAM systems. It solves the problem of maintaining a coherent 3D structure in real-time by inserting new Gaussians to capture newly visible scene elements and pruning those that are geometrically unstable or contribute negligibly to the render. Unlike static densification strategies that rely on gradient magnitude thresholds, this method relies on visibility consistency across a sliding window of keyframes and opacity thresholds to manage the primitive set. See [Gaussian Insertion and Pruning](10074-gaussian-insertion-and-pruning.md).
 
 ### [Error-guided Guided Sampling](15896-error-guided-guided-sampling.md)
-Error-guided Guided Sampling (EMS) is a densification strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that targets regions of high perceptual error rather than relying solely on gradient magnitude or opacity thresholds. It identifies low-SSIM training views, computes a pixel-wise error mask, and spawns new Gaussians specifically in high-error areas while using depth information and spatial masks to prevent overlapping placements and edge artifacts [15896-error-guided-guided-sampling]. Unlike standard densification which refines existing primitives based on local gradients, EMS performs a global view-based error analysis to insert new primitives where the current representation fails to reconstruct the image structure [15896-error-guided-guided-sampling].
+A densification strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that targets regions of high perceptual error rather than relying solely on gradient magnitude or opacity thresholds. It identifies low-SSIM training views, computes a pixel-wise error mask, and spawns new Gaussians specifically in high-error areas while using depth information and spatial masks to prevent overlapping placements and edge artifacts. Unlike standard densification which refines existing primitives based on local gradients, EMS performs a global view-based error analysis to insert new primitives where the current representation fails to reconstruct the image structure. See [Error-guided Guided Sampling](15896-error-guided-guided-sampling.md).
 
