 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive densification in 3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced alongside the original 3DGS paper by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S1][S6, unofficial]. The original method used gradient magnitude and opacity thresholds to split and prune Gaussians every 100 iterations. Subsequent research has expanded this family to address specific limitations:
-*   **Memory Efficiency:** Methods like Mini-Splatting [S1] and A LoD of Gaussians [S2, unofficial] introduced constrained budgets and parallel respawn strategies to reduce VRAM usage and handle ultra-large scenes.
-*   **SLAM Integration:** Gaussian Insertion and Pruning [10074-gaussian-insertion-and-pruning] and Rendering-Guided Densification [S5] adapted the strategy for real-time SLAM, using visibility windows and depth consistency instead of batch gradients.
-*   **Error-Driven Refinement:** EMS [15896-error-guided-guided-sampling] and Score-based Densification [4705-score-based-densification] shifted focus from local gradients to global perceptual error and user-defined budgets.
-*   **Performance Optimization:** Variants like I3DGS [S3, unofficial] and Global-to-local frameworks [S6, unofficial] optimized the frequency and type of densification operations to accelerate training convergence.
+The concept of adaptive densification originated with the introduction of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by Kerbl et al. (2023), which established the baseline heuristic of splitting Gaussians with high positional gradients and pruning those with low opacity [S1, Parent]. Subsequent research has diversified this family to address specific limitations:
+*   **Memory Efficiency:** Methods like Mini-Splatting introduced constrained densification and blur splitting to reduce Gaussian counts while maintaining quality [S1].
+*   **SLAM Integration:** Gaussian Insertion and Pruning adapted densification for real-time SLAM, using visibility windows instead of global gradient accumulation [S1].
+*   **Perceptual Quality:** Error-guided sampling shifted focus from local gradients to global perceptual error (SSIM) to target structural artifacts [S1].
+*   **Training Speed:** Global-to-local frameworks and parallel respawning strategies aimed to reduce computational overhead during the early stages of training [S2, S6, unofficial].
 
